WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
Let's talk about a problem you might have when listening to English, especially when watching a movie.

00:07.000 --> 00:14.000
You don't understand what you hear, and this is not because you don't know the vocabulary.

00:14.000 --> 00:20.000
If you read the transcript or the subtitles, you will understand the material just fine.

00:20.000 --> 00:26.000
So you already know the words being used, you just don't recognize them when listening.

00:26.000 --> 00:28.000
So what should you do?

00:28.000 --> 00:38.000
According to many research studies, an effective way to deal with this problem is to practice listening through easy to hard training.

00:38.000 --> 00:46.000
In one study, participants were trained to perform a task related to hearing, and there were three levels of difficulty.

00:46.000 --> 00:49.000
Easy, normal, and difficult.

00:49.000 --> 00:58.000
The participants were divided into two groups. The first group was trained to perform only the difficult version of that task.

00:58.000 --> 01:06.000
The second group, however, started with the easy and normal versions first, before performing the difficult version.

01:06.000 --> 01:12.000
The researchers wanted to know which group would perform the difficult version better.

01:12.000 --> 01:14.000
So what was the result?

01:14.000 --> 01:22.000
Well, despite having less training with the difficult version, the second group actually performed better.

01:22.000 --> 01:37.000
Practically speaking, this fighting suggests that if you have difficulty doing something, instead of just keep doing that, it's more effective to start with something easy and then slowly increase the difficulty as you improve.

01:37.000 --> 01:41.000
So how can you apply this knowledge to improve your listening?

01:41.000 --> 01:58.000
I'll tell you. First, you have to make it easy in the beginning. For example, if you listen to podcasts in English on your smartphone, find an app that lets you chain playback speed so that you can slow down or speed up what you're listening to.

01:58.000 --> 02:03.000
If you listen on your computer, there are programs that can do that as well.

02:03.000 --> 02:10.000
Then, whenever you have trouble understanding, use the app to slow down the audio like this.

02:10.000 --> 02:21.000
I told my mom that I was going to win first place, and she just laughed at me. To be honest, she had no real reason to believe me, because I wasn't a particularly great speller, and I hadn't even placed in the previous year's contest.

02:21.000 --> 02:26.000
But for some reason, I was so sure of myself. So I said, okay mom.

02:26.000 --> 02:33.000
But it gets easier, right? You have more time to think, you have more time to make out words that unpronounce clearly.

02:33.000 --> 02:37.000
You have more time to get the meanings of new words and phrases.

02:37.000 --> 02:41.000
The idea here is to make it easy in the beginning.

02:41.000 --> 02:53.000
Listen at slower speeds, listen to speakers that are easy to understand, listen to easy stuff like podcasts and interviews as opposed to movies, start small.

02:53.000 --> 03:00.000
But you're not done yet. Easy to hard training requires you to go from easy to difficult.

03:00.000 --> 03:06.000
So once you're listening skills improve, you have to gradually increase the difficulty.

03:06.000 --> 03:16.000
For example, if you're listening to a speaker that speaks slowly and you can understand everything, instead of listening at the normal speed, speed it up like this.

03:17.000 --> 03:23.000
Imagine you're going to a party, and you go to a party and you're meeting some new people you've never met before.

03:23.000 --> 03:27.000
And at that party, you get a certain feeling of each person you're connecting with.

03:27.000 --> 03:30.000
You go, oh, over here, here's a woman who's like, very, very curious and outgoing and fun-loving.

03:30.000 --> 03:32.000
Oh, yeah, I'd love to connect with her. She's like, she's like a lot of fun.

03:32.000 --> 03:40.000
Now, after you speed it up, you must still be able to understand the content, but you should feel a little uncomfortable.

03:40.000 --> 03:45.000
This will help you become more familiar with listening to fast speakers.

03:45.000 --> 03:53.000
The idea here is to keep pushing yourself if you keep listening to something easy, your listening skills will stop improving.

03:53.000 --> 03:56.000
So you have to keep challenging yourself.

03:56.000 --> 04:05.000
So listen at faster speeds. Also, don't keep listening to the same person, listen to different people with different speaking styles.

04:05.000 --> 04:10.000
And then, start watching movies without subtitles. It is still difficult.

04:10.000 --> 04:18.000
You can slow down the movie by 5% or 10%, or you can slow it down even more if you don't mind the slowness.

04:18.000 --> 04:24.000
Once you get used to it, you can then start watching movies at normal speed.

